 is clearly worse.","points":["Pins the bishop on f3 to the rook.","Moving away uncovers the bishop on b7, which now hits the bishop on f3.","The next best move, 17…f5, would drop Black's winning chances from 51% to 34% (+1.85).","17…Nd6 gets the knight out of the way of Nxe4.","White was threatening Nxe4, winning a knight. The knight steps away.","White answers 18.Bxb7, taking the bishop on b7, leaving Black with no bishop pair."]},{"ply":62,"name":"31…Ra5","cls":"mistake","color":"b","headline":"31…Ra5 is a mistake: Black's winning chances go from 47% to 30%.","points":["White answers 32.c4, which pushes the passed pawn to c4.","Better was 31…Ra1+ (+0.34): check, and White has only one legal reply."],"better":"31…Ra1+ gives check, leaving one reply. Engine line: 32.Kb2 Rh1 33.Nd7+ Ke7 34.Nb8+ Kd6 (+0.34)."},{"ply":63,"name":"32.Nc4","cls":"miss","color":"w","headline":"32.Nc4 misses the chance 31…Ra5 gave: c4 would have punished it. White's winning chances go from 70% to 55%.","points":["Black answers 32…Rf5: the rook takes the open f-file.","Better was 32.c4 (+2.25), which pushes the passed pawn to c4."],"better":"32.c4 pushes the passed pawn to c4. Engine line: 32…Ra6 33.Nd7+ Ke8 34.Ne5 Ra5 35.Nd3 (+2.25)."}],"opening":{"name":"Indian Defense: Gibbins-Weidenhagen Gambit","eco":"A45","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 2.g4: 3 moves in all, 2 by White and 1 by Black.","Theory runs out after 2.g4: too few games between strong players reached this position to go further."]}}